/**
@defgroup schema structs as Packed CBOR, with fields at fixed offsets

`cbor::schema<T>` writes a struct as one Packed CBOR message
(draft-ietf-cbor-packed-19). Each field of fixed size has an offset that
the compiler computes. A read goes to that offset. It does not walk the
fields before it. Include `<cbor/schema.hpp>`, or `<cbor/cbor.hpp>`,
which includes it.

A decoder of Packed CBOR reads the message as a map. The keys of the map
are the names of the members. `cbor::schema<T>` reads only a message of
`T`. A message of another struct gives `incorrect_type`.

`cbor::schema<T>` reads the description of each struct from
`cbor::describe_members<T>`. It builds with C++23 on g++ 15, g++ 16,
clang 23 and AppleClang. With C++26 reflection (g++ 16 with
`-std=c++26 -freflection`), it also checks each description against the
public members.

Use `cbor::schema` for messages that your own programs write and read. Use
`cbor::structs` for CBOR that any program writes, mapped into the members
of a struct or a class.

@section schema_describe THE DESCRIPTION

Each struct has one tag. Each member has one key, and each key is a
text. `CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE(type, tag, members...)` writes the description of
a struct at global scope. `CBOR_DEFINE_TYPE_MEMBERS(type, tag, names...)`
writes it from the names of the members. Each name is the key of its
member.

- `CBOR_MEMBER(type, member)` takes the name of the member as the key.
- `cbor::member{"x", &type::member}` takes the text `x` as the key.
- The macros write a specialization of `cbor::describe_members`.
  `cbor::structs` reads the same specialization.

These do not compile:

- a struct with no description, or with `cbor::untagged` as its tag;
- a struct in an anonymous namespace that no code uses. clang refuses it
  with `-Wunused-const-variable`;
- two structs of `T` with one tag number, the alternatives of a variant
  included;
- a tag number that a standard type uses: 0, 1, 2, 3, 24, 64 to 87, 100,
  258, 1004 and 32873;
- a tag number that the IANA registry calls always invalid: 21334, 65535,
  4294967295 and 18446744073709551615;
- a key that is not a text;
- two members with one key;
- a member that the description lists twice;
- a member that the description does not list. With reflection, the
  check uses the public members. Without reflection, Boost.PFR counts the
  members of an aggregate where it is available, and a count that
  differs does not compile.

A type whose name holds a comma, such as `pair<int, int>`, takes an alias
in the macro. The preprocessor splits the arguments of a macro at each
comma.

@section schema_base CLASSES WITH A BASE CLASS

A class with a base class is described with its base. Use
`CBOR_DEFINE_DERIVED_TYPE_MEMBERS(type, base, tag, members...)`, or
`using base = B;` in `cbor::describe_members<T>`. The description then
lists the members of the base too.

With reflection, the named base must be the one direct base of the
class. A base class that the description does not name does not compile.
Without reflection, the named base is accepted when it is a base class
of the type. The check also accepts an indirect base.

@section schema_variant THE VARIANT MEMBERS

A `std::variant` member of a described class takes these alternatives:

- a class with a description and a tag;
- a number, a `bool`, or an enum with a fixed underlying type;
- a text or a byte string.

A list, a map, an array, an optional, a variant, and `std::monostate` do
not compile as alternatives. A list, a map, an array or an optional of
a variant does not compile. Each alternative occurs once.

The CBOR head of an item chooses its alternative. Two alternatives whose
items begin with one head do not compile. Examples are `std::int32_t`
with `std::uint32_t`, `float` with `std::bfloat16_t`, an enum with its
underlying type, and two text types. The class alternatives name one
direct base class, and it is the same base for each of them.

@section schema_write WRITE A MESSAGE

- `encode(value)` gives a `std::expected<std::string, cbor::error>`.
- `encode(value, target)` appends the message to `target`. It gives the
  count of bytes that it appended. `target` is a `std::string`, a
  container of `char`, a `std::span<char>` or a message writer.
- A `std::span<char>` that is shorter than the message gives
  `no_buffer_space`.

Only public members are written. Each field has a head of fixed width,
so a value does not change the offset of a field. A float is written at
the width of its type, and its bits are copied.

A list of fixed-size structs is written with one copy where the struct in
memory has the bytes of the element. A compile-time probe finds the layout.
Otherwise each element is written field by field.

@section schema_at_path READ ONE VALUE

`cbor::schema<T>::at_path<Path>(bytes, indexes...)` reads one value with
no accessor. The value is a number, a `bool`, an enum, or a
`std::optional` of one. It gives a `std::expected` of the value.

A path starts with `$`. A part `.name` names a member. A part `[n]` names
an element. A part `[]` takes its index from the next argument, a
`std::size_t`. The count
of arguments equals the count of `[]`. The path is not JSONPath. It has
no wildcard, no filter and no descendant segment.

On a byte string, a `std::array` or a `std::vector` of `std::uint8_t` or
`std::byte`, `[n]` reads one byte.

These do not compile: a member that `T` does not have, a wrong count of
arguments, a path to a text, and a path to a member that is not
trivially copyable, such as a `std::vector` of structs.

@section schema_path OPEN A MESSAGE

`cbor::schema<T>::%path(...)` opens a message and gives the root accessor,
`cbor::schema<T>::accessor<T>`. It checks the message once, and it reads
no field. The forms are:

- `%path(std::move(bytes))`: the string moves into the owner. Nothing is
  copied.
- `%path(owner)`: `owner` is a `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`. The
  message is read in place.
- `%path(owner, part)`: `part` is a `std::string_view` into memory that
  `owner`, a `std::shared_ptr<void const>`, keeps alive.

An accessor holds its owner. Each value and each accessor that it gives
holds the same owner. So a child accessor stays valid after the root
accessor ends.

@section schema_accessor READ WITH AN ACCESSOR

`at_path<Path>(indexes...)` of an accessor reads a part of the message. A
path that starts with `$` starts at the root. A path that starts with
`@` starts at the part that the accessor names. The result is a
`std::expected`:

- a number, a `bool` or an enum gives the value;
- a text or a byte string gives a `std::string_view` into the message;
- a struct, a list or a map gives its accessor;
- a `std::optional` of a type E gives a `std::optional` of the result of E.

`size()` gives the count of a list or a map. `view<Path>()` gives a
`cbor::typed_array_view` of a list of numbers. Only the root accessor has
`view`.

A `std::string_view` stays valid while an accessor of the same owner
lives. Keep the accessor in a variable. `at_path` on a temporary
accessor does not compile. A call on an accessor that was moved from
throws `std::logic_error`.

A `std::variant` member has `index()`, which gives the index of the held
alternative, and `get<A>()`. `get<A>()` gives a class alternative as its
accessor. It gives a number, `bool` or enum as its value. It gives a text
or a byte string as a `std::string_view`. Another alternative gives
`incorrect_type`. A path cannot end at a variant member.

A list of fixed-size structs gives a random access range of element
accessors. In an element, `@` reads a member of the element. `$` starts
at the root.

@section schema_typed_array READ A LIST OF NUMBERS

`typed_array_view<E>` is a random access, sized range over a list of
numbers in the message. Each element is a value of `E`, read in little
endian. The view holds the owner of the bytes.

- `at`, `first` and `last` give an index error as a value.
- `operator[]`, `front` and `back` throw `std::logic_error` outside the
  view.
- `begin` and `end` on a temporary view do not compile.

@section schema_decode READ THE WHOLE STRUCT

`cbor::schema<T>::%decode(...)` reads the whole message into a `T`. It
gives a `std::expected<cbor::ref<T>, cbor::error>`. The `cbor::ref` holds
the owner of the bytes, and no form copies the bytes. An rvalue
`std::string` moves into a new owner. A `std::shared_ptr<std::string const>`
is read in place. A `std::shared_ptr<void const>` with a view of
the bytes is read in place too.

- A struct with a default constructor is made with it. Then each
  described member is set.
- A class with no default constructor is made by a constructor. That
  constructor takes the described members in the order of the
  description.
- The forms with a `target` take the start value. A `std::pmr` container
  with its memory resource gives the result its memory. These forms need
  a default constructor of `T`.

@section schema_apart STORE THE DESCRIPTION APART

Each call takes `cbor::description_place` as a template argument. The
default is `inside`, and the message holds the description of its
structs.

With `cbor::description_place::apart`, `encode` writes the message
without the description. Each read form reads such a message only with
`apart`. A read with `apart` of a message that has the description gives
`incorrect_type`.

`cbor::schema<T>::description()` gives the description. It is the same
in every message of `T`. The size of a message written with `apart` plus
the size of `description()` is the size of the message with the
description. A generic decoder of Packed CBOR does not read a message
written with `apart`.

@section schema_unverified READ A MESSAGE FROM A PEER YOU TRUST

`unverified_path`, `unverified_at_path` and `unverified_decode` take the
same arguments as `%path`, `at_path` and `%decode`. They check each bound
of the message, so no read leaves the message. They do not check the
data of the message. A wrong byte gives a wrong value, not an error.

These forms do not check:

- the tag of each struct;
- the head of a number, a `bool`, a fixed string or a `std::array`;
- the head byte of a text, a list or a map, and the tag of a typed array;
- the prefix of a list of fixed-size structs;
- the start of the table, and the fillers before the items;
- that the length of a typed array is a multiple of its element size;
- that a `bool` byte is 0 or 1. Any other byte reads as `true`;
- in `unverified_decode`, the order of the references and of the items.

These forms check:

- that each head, length and item lies inside the message;
- that each directory entry lies between the start and the end of the
  items;
- each index against the count of its list, table or directory;
- the limits of the build and, in `unverified_decode`, the nesting depth.

Every accessor of `unverified_path` is unverified too.

@section schema_size SIZE AND OFFSET

`cbor::schema<T>::fixed_size()` gives the count of bytes of the root
record of `T`. The root record ends the message. `fixed_size<U>()` gives
the count of bytes that a field of type `U` takes.

- A number takes its head and its argument, both of fixed width.
- A struct takes its class tag, its table reference, a record head of 5
  bytes and its fields.
- A text, a list, a map, an optional of variable size, or a
  `std::variant` takes a reference `6(N)` of 6 bytes.

A type with no fixed size does not compile. `fixed_size` gives no error
at run time.

`cbor::item_size` of a message gives the size of the message.

`cbor::schema<T>::member_offset<&T::m>()` gives the offset of the head of
the member `m` from the start of its record. The fields follow the key
order of RFC 8949, section 4.2.1: a shorter key comes first, then the
bytes of the key. This order is not the order of the declarations.

@section schema_wire THE WIRE FORMAT

A message is tag 113 (Basic Packed CBOR) on an array of two items: a
table and a rump.

- The table holds the record function, tag 114, of each struct. The
  record function gives the keys of one struct. For each struct that is
  the element of a list of fixed-size structs, the table holds a
  description. The description is the class tag and the key and the
  RFC 8746 type of each member.
- The table holds a directory of 4-byte offsets of the parts of variable
  size.
- The fillers are `undefined` items. They place the parts at index 16 or
  later.
- The parts hold the texts, the byte strings, the lists, the maps and the
  items of the variant members.
- The rump is the root record. It stands at the end of the message.

A record starts with its class tag. A reference to its record function
follows. The first eight references are tags 128 to 135. Later references
are tag 6 on an array of two. Then an array with a count of 4 bytes holds
the fields. Each field has a head of fixed width. A field of variable size
is `6(N)`, a reference with a 4-byte `N` into the table.

A `std::variant` member is a field `6(N)`. Its part is the item of the
alternative that the variant holds. The head of the item names the
alternative.

A list of a number is an RFC 8746 typed array in little endian. A
`std::vector<double>` is tag 86.

A list of fixed-size structs is `32873([simple(j), h'...'])`. `j` is the
index of its description in the table. From index 16 on, `6(N)` takes
the place of `simple(j)`. The byte string holds the elements with no
padding, in little endian. Tag 32873 is provisional. The IANA registry
does not have it yet.

@section schema_cost COST

`%path` with a shared owner takes a time that does not depend on the size
of the message. `%path` with an rvalue takes the same time, and it makes
one allocation for the owner. Each `at_path` takes one step for each part
of its path. `%decode` and `encode` take a time in the size of the message.

An error of `%path`, `at_path` or `%decode` can cost a walk over the
message. The walk checks whether the message ends too early.

@section schema_errors ERRORS

- `too_little_data`: the message ends too early. `%path`, `at_path` and
  `%decode` give this error also where another check fails first. A
  message that is shorter than its table and root gives it in `%path`.
- `incorrect_type`: a message of another struct or layout, a wrong tag of
  a struct, a wrong head of a field, a variant item whose head names no
  alternative, or `get<A>()` of another alternative.
- `index_out_of_bounds`: an index past the end of a list.
- `unpopulated_table_index` and `syntax_error`: a reference or a
  directory entry that is broken.
- `inadmissible_type_for_tag_content`: a typed array, or a list of
  fixed-size structs, whose length is not a multiple of its element size.
- `nesting_depth_exceeded`: a value that is nested too deep for `encode`
  or for `%decode`.
- `not_enough_memory`: an allocation of the owner in `%path` fails, or an
  allocation of the result in `%decode` fails.
- `no_buffer_space`: a `std::span<char>` is shorter than the message.
- `value_too_large`: the message is longer than 2^32 - 1 bytes.

A null or empty owner throws `std::logic_error` in `%path`, `%decode` and
the unverified forms. A call on a moved-from accessor throws it too.
`operator[]`, `front` and `back` of a typed array view throw it outside
the view.

The unverified forms give the error of the check that fails.

@manonly
.SH "EXAMPLES"
@endmanonly
@snippet doc_reference_schema.cpp schema group
*/
